<p>Dear Students interested in Studio Printmaking Winter,  OR Studio and Advanced Printmaking Spring 2027,</p><p>If you are interested in applying to take this course in the Winter or either course in Spring of 2027, please read this carefully</p><p>This academic year I will offer Studio Printmaking in the Winter and Spring Terms. </p><p>I am asking <b>everyone</b> who wishes to join the class, <b>even if you contacted me previously</b>, to respond by Friday, October 16 by 5pm to each of the questions below.  The course description is on the website.</p><p><b>This 'question and answer' application helps you understand that it is a course of specialized study immersed in the craft of how to create relief and intaglio prints, by hand, using tools and materials within a set of pre-determined studio protocols.</p><p>It requires a unique mindfulness for craft, a respect for materials and instruction, and an openness to learning processes that are unfamiliar and interdependent.</p><p>Its structure may not suit everyone - it is designed so you can learn how to use tools and materials.  The results are up to you, how you interact with, engage within, dream the metal/wood/ink/paper.</p><p>Answer the following questions so I might be able to determine that it is a course that makes sense for you, someone who wishes to expand their art practice.  It is similar in nature to concentrated advanced study in other fields - it takes patience and work.</p><ol><li>You need to attend the first class and you need to attend regularly for the entire 10-week term - there are no exceptions for late arrivals to campus - hybrid instruction is not available; if you plan on arriving after the beginning of classes, you won't be eligible for the class.  Do you understand this and are you able to commit to being present?</li><li>What are the other studio art courses you have taken here at COA or other colleges, beyond high school classes?  If you haven't taken a college-level studio art course, why do you think you are eligible to take this course?</li><li>Do you regularly attend your classes?  Do you arrive on time for class?</li><li>Although you can work from an imaginative place unique to your own self, will you be able to commit to a structured program of technical instruction in the studio?</li><li>Why do you want to take Studio Printmaking?</li><li>If you cannot in the Winter of 2027, would you take this class in the Spring of 2027?</li><li>During this Academic year, 2026-2027, what year are you 1st, 2nd, 3rd or 4th year?</li></ol></p><p>Plan your travel to attend the entire term, if you want to take the course.</p><p>Many thanks for taking your time to answer these questions and have a great weekend. All best Catherine</p><p>PS</p><p>The following further describes the nature of the courses.<ol><li>Students follow a technical course of study that respects traditions of century old practices of relief and intaglio workshops.  Each week new skills and strategies will be taught – these build on each other from one week to the next.</li><li>If you do not like working within a specific framework that allows expression and attends to a system that may be challenging, then it might be that the course is not a good match for you.</li><li>Working with fine paper, inks, small batch chemicals, and equipment in a Printmaking Studio is like working within a Bronze Foundry, the medium values innovation in expression but asks for respect for process and attentiveness to it.</li><li>If you have an affinity for it, the next course, Advanced Printmaking expands the range of experimentation.  The studio is used in this context.  It is not an open use facility.  You must take Studio Printmaking to take Advanced Printmaking, and Advanced Printmaking to take an Independent Study in Printmaking.</li></ol></p>